    <briefdescription>
<para>Process-environment get/set wrappers used by configuration sections. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>This file contains the two small functions declared in <ref refid="envvars_8hpp" kindref="compound">envvars.hpp</ref>. One reads a name/value pair from the operating system&apos;s environment; the other writes (or overwrites) one. Both operate on plain text and leave any type conversion to the class above them. Errors while writing are reported and swallowed rather than thrown.</para>
<para>WHY THIS FILE MATTERS IN THE SERVER: This is the lowest level of the configuration stack: the only code that touches the C library functions std::getenv and setenv directly. <ref refid="classEnvLoader" kindref="compound">EnvLoader</ref> calls get() every time a configuration section is built, so this file is what actually makes environment-driven configuration possible. If it were missing, no other file could discover the deployment&apos;s settings, and the server could only rely on hard-coded defaults.</para>
<para>CONCEPTS TO LEARN (for beginners):<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Environment variables: <ulink url="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/environ.7.html">https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/environ.7.html</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>std::getenv / std::setenv: <ulink url="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/program/getenv">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/program/getenv</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>std::optional (a value that may be missing): <ulink url="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/optional">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/optional</ulink></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><simplesect kind="author"><para>Amr Osman <ulink url="mailto:dr3mro@gmail.com">dr3mro@gmail.com</ulink> </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="date"><para>2026-11-16 </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
